<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Demographic categories and values used to generate agent personas. Note that the <span id="S3.T2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Region of Origin</span> category corresponds to regions of the United States.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct two sets of experiments: (1) qualitative experiments (Section 4.1 and 4.2) as a proof-of-concept on LLMs’ internal consistency on preferences and openness, and (2) formalized experiments (Section 4.3) to compare various LLMs’ performances for robustness. For both experiment sets, we use <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3.1-8B-Instruct</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">et al., 2024</a>)</cite> as the judge (except in cases where we evaluate <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3.1-8B-Instruct</span> as the agent model, in which case we use <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen et al., 2025</a>)</cite> as the judge). For the qualitative experiments below, we use <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct</span> as the agent model. For the formalized and aggregated experiments, we examine <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5</span> models with various sizes (3B, 7B, and 14B) as well as other model types (<span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3.1-8B</span>, <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemma-2-9B</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2024</a>)</cite>, <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Mistral-7B</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Olmo-7B</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Groeneveld et al., 2024</a>)</cite>) as the agents.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Scope and Generalization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we deliberately narrow the scope of our experiments to maintain control and clarity. Specifically, we focus only on two dimensions of behavior for each agent: Openness (<math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) and Preference (<math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>). We further restrict our analysis to agents representing individuals from the United States, using the demographic attributes listed in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.4 Experiment Set-ups ‣ 3 A Framework for Probing Behavioral Coherence ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Finally, when examining conversations, we limit ourselves to the set of topics defined in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.4 Experiment Set-ups ‣ 3 A Framework for Probing Behavioral Coherence ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">That said, our framework can be extended to explore other forms of behavioral inconsistency in LLM agents. To study different internal states, one could simply substitute alternative question sets in place of those we used for Openness and Preferences. For example, to capture traits like Agreeableness or Neuroticism. Demographic coverage could be broadened by adjusting the system prompts that define each agent. Likewise, the set of conversational topics can be adapted to align with any new traits or demographic variations under consideration.
Although we leave such extensions to future work, we believe that any combination of traits is likely to exhibit behavioral inconsistencies of the kind we observe here.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Results</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we aim to uncover the discrepancy between the <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">appearance</span> of behavioral consistency and its breakdown under closer examination. To organize our discussion, we analyze results along two dimensions: <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">preference</span> and <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">openness</span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>On Preferences</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Finding #1: Increasing Preference Gap Decreases Agreement Score</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The most direct test of consistency is whether agents with similar preferences are more likely to agree than those with divergent ones. We compute the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">preference gap</span> as the absolute difference in two agents’ preference scores (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|1-5|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|1-5|</annotation></semantics></math>). A gap of four indicates maximal disagreement in stated preference, while a gap of zero indicates identical views.
As expected, agreement decreases with larger preference gaps. Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Finding #1: Increasing Preference Gap Decreases Agreement Score ‣ 4.1 On Preferences ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows that pairs with aligned preferences (gap = 0) achieve the highest agreement, while pairs with greater gaps yield progressively lower scores.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.03736v1/figures/result_pref_base.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:274/225;" width="274" height="225" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Average agreement score when conversation pairs are grouped according to the gap in their preference</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Stopping the analysis here would misleadingly suggest that agents are behaviorally consistent. In fact, the results in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Finding #1: Increasing Preference Gap Decreases Agreement Score ‣ 4.1 On Preferences ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> conceal significant inconsistencies. A deeper analysis reveals three systematic discrepancies: (1) disagreement is strongly dampened between agents with dissimilar preferences, (2) shared negative sentiment produces weaker alignment than shared positive sentiment, and (3) topic contentiousness exerts undue influence on outcomes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Finding #2: Agreement amplifies, disagreement dampens:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Finding #1: Increasing Preference Gap Decreases Agreement Score ‣ 4.1 On Preferences ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows that agreement levels remain high even at large preference gaps. Instead of converging toward the minimum score of 1, pairs with maximal divergence average between 3.5 and 3.7. Fewer than 1% of all conversations yield scores below 3. In other words, agents virtually never disagree, no matter how much they diverge in their preferences. Crucially, this is despite the fact that we have calibrated our LLM-as-a-judge to respond via its system prompt appropriately for each potential score (1-5).
One way to interpret these patterns is as an instance of sycophancy, a bias in which language models tend to be overly agreeable toward their interlocutor. In the context of this study, it would manifest as a systematic amplification of agreement and a corresponding suppression of disagreement.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row"></th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observed Probability</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expected Probability</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row">Agreement Score</th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column">Preference Gap = 0</th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column">Preference Gap = 4</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">1</th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">0.000204</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">0.371</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row">2</th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">0.00163</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">0.423</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row">3</th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">0.205</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">0.205</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row">4</th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">0.423</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">0.00163</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">5</th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">0.371</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">0.000204</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>To estimate how much preference is being depressed, we assume that the expected disagreement pattern for pairs with the widest preference gap is the inverse of pairs with a preference gap of zero.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">How suppressed is disagreement?
We estimate this by adopting a simplifying assumption–agents should, in principle, disagree at the same rate as they agree.
We already know one end of this spectrum, namely, the amount of agreement when agents are perfectly aligned (i.e., those with a preference gap of zero). We establish the other end of the spectrum, the disagreement side, by assuming it to be the inverse of agreement.
Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Finding #2: Agreement amplifies, disagreement dampens: ‣ 4.1 On Preferences ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> compares the observed and expected distributions for pairs with a preference gap of four by inverting the agreement probabilities observed when the gap is zero. Applying bootstrap sampling, we derive the counterfactual outcomes shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Finding #2: Agreement amplifies, disagreement dampens: ‣ 4.1 On Preferences ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For pairs with a preference gap of four, the observed mean agreement score (3.6) is roughly twice the expected value (1.8), revealing substantial suppression of disagreement. This effect extends across smaller gaps as well. Assuming the expected preference of Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Finding #2: Agreement amplifies, disagreement dampens: ‣ 4.1 On Preferences ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and that the probability distributions shifts linearly from Preference Gap 0 probabilities to Preference Gap 4 probabilities, the differences between expected and observed means are 1.8, 1.27, 0.89, and 0.55 for gaps four through one, corresponding to suppression magnitudes of 2.0, 1.53, 1.30, and 1.15 respectively. Replicating the analysis with a sigmoidal shift between a Preference Gap of 0 and Preference Gap of 4 yields the same conclusion: disagreement is consistently depressed across all preference gaps, with no symmetry around a neutral midpoint of 3.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.03736v1/figures/result_pref_no_disagreement.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/274;" width="548" height="274" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Estimates for disagreement suppression where the observed and expected agreement distributions are compared across preference gaps. Each row corresponds to the difference in preference on the topic of interest. The first two columns use the entire sample, but differ in the way the means shift across preference gaps. The third column is based on a subsample where the agents’ topic bias is set to <math id="S4.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, this suppression persists even when agents are explicitly instructed to adopt firm positions (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}=2</annotation></semantics></math>). As shown in the third column of Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Finding #2: Agreement amplifies, disagreement dampens: ‣ 4.1 On Preferences ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, significant discrepancies remain between expected and observed distributions. An anomaly arises where the highest agreement occurs at a gap of one rather than zero, but the broader result is clear: disagreement is systematically dampened despite efforts to mitigate sycophancy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Finding #3: Shared sentiment, divergent speech destinations:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The patterns shown in the third column of Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Finding #2: Agreement amplifies, disagreement dampens: ‣ 4.1 On Preferences ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> point to a deeper inconsistency. Consider two pairs of agents: one strongly favors a topic, while the other strongly dislikes it. In principle, both pairs should exhibit high agreement—one through shared enthusiasm, the other through shared aversion. In other words, sentiment alignment, whether positive or negative, should yield comparable agreement outcomes. Yet this is not what we observe.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.03736v1/figures/result_pref_agree_less.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:274/274;" width="274" height="274" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Agreement scores for pairs with fixed sentiment anchors. One of the agents’ preference is fixed at 1 and 5 for the two groups respectively. The specific preference pairing is noted inside the distribution. Note the preference gap pairing of ’1-1’ has nearly the same agreement distribution as ’2-5’ in spite of complete agreement on the topic in question.
Pairs anchored at 1 systematically agree less than those anchored at 5, even under identical preference gaps.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To examine this, we decompose each preference gap level into two subgroups. In the first, one agent is fixed at a preference of one; in the second, one agent is fixed at five. If agents behaved consistently, the agreement distributions of these subgroups would overlap.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Instead, Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Finding #3: Shared sentiment, divergent speech destinations: ‣ 4.1 On Preferences ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows a systematic asymmetry. For example, at a gap of three, we compare pairs (1,4) and (2,5); at a gap of zero, the pairs are (1,1) and (5,5). Across all cases, pairs anchored at one consistently display lower agreement scores than those anchored at five. Moreover, this disparity grows as the preference gap narrows—from a mean difference of 0.33 at gap three to 0.62 at gap zero. Strikingly, the (1,1) pair aligns more closely with (2,5)—three preference levels apart—than with its supposed counterpart (5,5).
These results suggest that LLM agents systematically penalize expressions of strong negative sentiment. Even when preferences are fully aligned, as in (1,1), agreement fails to reach the level observed among positive anchors (5,5). In short, negative sentiment alignment is treated less consistently than positive alignment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Finding #4: Topic contentiousness trumps preference:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our third indicator of inconsistency concerns the undue influence of topic contentiousness on agreement outcomes. In principle, agreement or disagreement should reflect agents’ preferences rather than the inherent nature of the topic itself. While some subjects, such as taxes or immigration, are commonly perceived as polarizing, this reflects how individuals position themselves, not an intrinsic property of the topic. Even seemingly benign discussions, such as favorite sports or seasons, can become contentious if participants hold opposing views.
For this reason, our expectation is straightforward: pairs with the same sentiment should exhibit similar levels of agreement regardless of topic.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.03736v1/figures/result_pref_topic.png" id="S4.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:1500/1500;" width="1500" height="1500" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Full sample</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.03736v1/figures/result_pref_topic_b2.png" id="S4.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:1500/1500;" width="1500" height="1500" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Subsample, <math id="S4.F7.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}=2</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>The two graphs show whether topic controversy has an independent effect on agreement outcomes when preferences of conversation pairs are held constant. Gray bands display the confidence interval for the least controversial topics.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To test this, we disaggregated agents into preference pairs (e.g., (1,3), (2,4)) and calculated agreement scores at three levels of contentiousness: neutral (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=1</annotation></semantics></math>), medium (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=2</annotation></semantics></math>), and high (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=3</annotation></semantics></math>). Using neutral topics as a baseline, we then examined how agreement shifts as topics become more controversial.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figures <a href="#S4.F7.sf1" title="In Figure 7 ‣ Finding #4: Topic contentiousness trumps preference: ‣ 4.1 On Preferences ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(a)</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F7.sf2" title="In Figure 7 ‣ Finding #4: Topic contentiousness trumps preference: ‣ 4.1 On Preferences ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a>, agreement is strongly shaped by topic contentiousness. For the full sample, 11 of 15 preference pairs at <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=3</annotation></semantics></math> fall outside the neutral baseline’s confidence interval. Interestingly, scores dip below baseline for lower-ranked pairs (e.g., (1,3)) but rise above baseline for higher-ranked pairs (e.g., (3,4)).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This effect intensifies when focusing on agents instructed to take strong positions (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}=2</annotation></semantics></math>). In Figure <a href="#S4.F7.sf2" title="In Figure 7 ‣ Finding #4: Topic contentiousness trumps preference: ‣ 4.1 On Preferences ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a>, all six preference pairs diverge significantly from the neutral reference under medium and high contentiousness. In short, even when preferences are fixed and stances are reinforced, topic contentiousness continues to override preference alignment as the main driver of agreement outcomes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>On Openness</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">So far, we have focused on preferences as the primary dimension of internal state. We now turn to openness, which captures how readily an agent may be persuaded by its interlocutor. In principle, greater openness should translate into higher levels of agreement, particularly in cases where agents begin with divergent preferences.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Finding #5: Increasing Openness, Increasing Agreement:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We observe the same pattern here as well – surface-level consistency belies deeper behavioral inconsistencies, which only becomes visible upon closer scrutiny.
Figure <a href="#S4.F8.sf1" title="In Figure 8 ‣ Finding #5: Increasing Openness, Increasing Agreement: ‣ 4.2 On Openness ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(a)</span></a> shows a surface-level trend: as openness increases, average agreement also rises. This is consistent with expectations that more receptive agents should converge more readily with their partners.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F8.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.03736v1/figures/result_open_base.png" id="S4.F8.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:1500/1500;" width="1500" height="1500" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Full sample</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F8.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.03736v1/figures/result_open_subsample.png" id="S4.F8.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:1500/1500;" width="1500" height="1500" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Subsample – preference pair (1,5)</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Agreement scores for pairs grouped according to how open agents are. In panel (b), the mean of all interactions is calculated for each openness pairing where the preference pair is (1, 5). Each horizontal bar represents the mean difference in agreement score between a given openness pairing and the openness pairing of (0, 0).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Finding #6: High Preference Gap and Low Openness, Too Strong Agreement:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">However, when we isolate cases of maximal preference divergence (pairs (1,5)), the pattern weakens considerably. Figure <a href="#S4.F8.sf2" title="In Figure 8 ‣ Finding #5: Increasing Openness, Increasing Agreement: ‣ 4.2 On Openness ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(b)</span></a> compares openness pairings against the baseline (0,0), where both agents are stubborn. If openness were functioning as expected, all other pairings should yield higher agreement than this baseline. Instead, most differences are negative: only 6 of the 28 pairings produce greater agreement than the baseline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These findings highlight an important inconsistency. Although aggregate results suggest that openness drives higher agreement, closer inspection reveals that this effect does not hold when it matters most, namely, when agents begin from diametrically opposed positions. In such cases, openness fails to reliably increase agreement, exposing a gap between surface plausibility and deeper behavioral coherence.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Formalized &amp; Aggregated Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The qualitative findings above suggest systematic inconsistencies in how LLM agents realize their internal states. To formalize these results, we define six explicit tests of behavioral consistency. A model is considered to <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pass</span> a test if its outcomes align with expectations of internal coherence. We use a conservative threshold of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.01</annotation></semantics></math>, applying Bonferroni corrections where multiple comparisons are made.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Test 1: Increasing Preference Gap Decreases Agreement</span>. The first preference test discussed in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 On Preferences ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>. We perform a Pearson’s correlation test on the agreement score and preference gap to determine if a negative relationship exists between the two.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Test 2: Agreement Amplifies, Disagreement Dampens</span> We test whether the distribution of the agreement scores with preference gap of 4 has a distribution equivalent to that of the inverse of those with preference gap of 0.
We test this symmetry with a Kolmogorov–Smirnov test.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Test 3: Shared Sentiment, Divergent Speech Destinations</span> Pairs with identical negative preferences (1–1) should agree as much as pairs with identical positive preferences (5–5). We test whether (1–1) scores exceed those of mixed pairs (2–5, 3–5, 4–5) using Mann–Whitney U, with Bonferroni correction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Test 4: Topic Contentiousness Trumps Preference</span> We first first compute Mann-Whitney U tests independently for each preference pair and topic level pairing. For instance, we compute this test for the Preference pairing ’1-1’ when comparing the distributions for Topic Levels of ’1’ and ’2’. We repeat this for all possible Preference pairings and Topic Level pairings to see if they are from the same distribution. We then aggregate these using the Bonferroni correction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Test 5: Increasing Openness, Increasing Agreement Score</span> The first openness test discussed in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 On Openness ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. We perform a Pearson’s correlation test on the combined sum of each agents’ openness score with the agreement score to determine if there is a positive linear relationship between Openness and Agreement. All preference pairings are considered in this test.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">6.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Test 6: High Preference Gap and Low Openness, Too Strong Agreement</span> We test whether the agreement score distribution for the lowest Openness pairing and largest Preference pair difference ’1-5’ is smaller than the agreement scores for other pairings. Similar to Test 4, we compute each comparison separately, then perform a Bonferroni correction on a Mann-Whitney U test.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">From the summarized results of Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.3 Formalized &amp; Aggregated Results ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we see that all models exhibit success in Tests 1 and 5, demonstrating that increasing the preference gap lowers agreement, while increasing openness raises agreement, as expected. However, we find that many models exhibit similar, troubling results to those found in <span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct</span> above. No model succeeded in either Tests 2 or 3. There were some successful tests for 4 and 5; however, these use Bonferroni corrections over a large number of independent tests and are thus more conservative estimates.
Overall, these results show that while models capture broad, surface-level trends, they systematically fail tests requiring deeper internal coherence. Importantly, this pattern holds across model sizes and families, suggesting that such inconsistencies are not idiosyncratic but general properties of current LLMs.</p>
</div>
