<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Detailed Framework Description</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we detail the exact methodology (prompts, etc.) used within Section 3 of our paper.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(a) Topic Preparation</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.4 Experiment Set-ups ‣ 3 A Framework for Probing Behavioral Coherence ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the set of topics we explore within our study. We examine several topics at each level of contentiousness in order to examine the effect of contentiousness on both individual agent response as well as on agreement during interaction with other agents.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(b) Agent Construction with External Profiles</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As described in Section 3 of our original paper, agents are composed of both a demographic background (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>) as well as information relating to their bias towards the topic of discussion (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The possible demographics of the agent are described in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.4 Experiment Set-ups ‣ 3 A Framework for Probing Behavioral Coherence ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. An agent is composed of only one value from each trait category, and these values are used to construct the agent’s system prompt. For example, one such agent in our study would have as part of it’s system prompt <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">You are a man in their twenties from an urban part of the Midwestern United States. Your highest level of educational attainment is Some High School.</span> We use all possible combinations of the values in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.4 Experiment Set-ups ‣ 3 A Framework for Probing Behavioral Coherence ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> to construct the set of agents.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond the demographics portion of the agent prompt, we also prompt agents with a bias related to the topic of discussion. When the bias value is 0, we add no further information into the agent’s system prompt. Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ (b) Agent Construction with External Profiles ‣ Appendix A Detailed Framework Description ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the information added to agent system prompts when the bias level <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Table <a href="#A1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ (b) Agent Construction with External Profiles ‣ Appendix A Detailed Framework Description ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows the information added to agent system prompts when the bias level <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, for each agent produced with the combination of traits from Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.4 Experiment Set-ups ‣ 3 A Framework for Probing Behavioral Coherence ‣ Are LLM Agents Behaviorally Coherent? Latent Profiles for Social Simulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, there are five new agents made once adding bias information (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=1</annotation></semantics></math> and In Favor, <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=1</annotation></semantics></math> and Against, <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=2</annotation></semantics></math> and In Favor, <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=2</annotation></semantics></math> and Against).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In total, for a given topic, we have <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4*5*2*4*6)*5=4800" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4800</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4*5*2*4*6)*5=4800</annotation></semantics></math> unique agents, where the first component comes from possible demographic values and the second comes from the different bias values. The variation among these agents allows us to test across a broader range of preferences <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, openness scores <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> and agreement scores, <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To further ensure that the agent responses correspond to the demographic prompting we use, we also end each agent system prompt with <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Whenever you respond to prompts or questions, you should maintain consistency with these background details and viewpoints, grounding your answers in the lived experience and perspective of this individual.</span>.</p>
</div>
